Please find the patch-ready content below; it intentionally avoids including any executable script payloads and instead uses descriptive placeholders where appropriate.

--- Begin README snippet (add to README.md or SECURITY.md) ---

Testing XSS protections (sanitization and safe rendering)

Manual testing:
1. Start the server (e.g., npm start).
2. Use curl or Postman to POST to /comments with a JSON body { "content": "SOME_TEST_PAYLOAD" } where SOME_TEST_PAYLOAD includes potentially dangerous markup (for manual testing, use harmless placeholders).
3. GET /comments/:id to verify the stored content is the sanitized variant and does not contain executable script tags.
4. Open the UI and verify CommentHtml renders the sanitized content safely (no alert popups or script execution).

Automated testing:
- Server unit tests: verify the sanitize middleware removes disallowed tags and attributes and preserves allowed ones.
- Integration tests: POST content containing a typical XSS pattern, GET it back, and assert the stored content does not include disallowed tags or dangerous attribute values (e.g., javascript: URIs).
- Client tests: render CommentHtml with server-sanitized HTML and assert the rendered DOM does not include script elements.

Policies and notes:
- Always sanitize on the server before storing. If you must keep raw input for audit, encrypt and restrict access; never send raw input to clients.
- Add Content-Security-Policy headers to reduce impact of any slip-through.
- Keep sanitize-html and other libs up to date.

--- End README snippet ---

--- Begin Jest test examples (create under tests/sanitize.test.js) ---

// tests/sanitize.test.js
const sanitizeHtml = require('sanitize-html');
const sanitizeMiddleware = require('../server/middleware/sanitize');

describe('Sanitize middleware', () => {
  test('removes script tags and unsafe attributes', () => {
    const raw = 'Text with <script>evil()</script> and an <a href="javascript:alert(1)">evil link</a>';
    const clean = sanitizeHtml(raw, {
      allowedTags: ['a', 'p', 'b', 'i', 'em', 'strong', 'ul', 'ol', 'li'],
      allowedAttributes: { a: ['href', 'rel', 'target'] }
    });
    // Assert that script tag is removed
    expect(clean).not.toMatch(/<script/i);
    // Assert javascript: link was removed or normalized (depends on your transform)
    expect(clean).not.toMatch(/javascript:/i);
  });

  test('preserves allowed tags and attributes', () => {
    const raw = '<p><strong>Bold</strong> and <a href="https://example.com" target="_blank">link</a></p>';
    const clean = sanitizeHtml(raw, {
      allowedTags: ['a', 'p', 'b', 'i', 'em', 'strong', 'ul', 'ol', 'li'],
      allowedAttributes: { a: ['href', 'rel', 'target'] }
    });
    expect(clean).toContain('<strong>Bold</strong>');
    expect(clean).toContain('href="https://example.com"');
  });
});

--- End Jest test examples ---

--- Begin Integration test example (supertest) ---

// tests/integration/comments.integration.test.js
const request = require('supertest');
const express = require('express');
const bodyParser = require('body-parser');
const commentsRouter = require('../../routes/comments'); // adjust path if needed

describe('Comments integration', () => {
  let app;
  beforeAll(() => {
    app = express();
    app.use(bodyParser.json());
    app.use('/comments', commentsRouter);
  });

  test('POST/GET flow returns sanitized content', async () => {
    const payload = { content: 'Hello <script>alert(1)</script> <b>ok</b>' };
    const postRes = await request(app).post('/comments').send(payload);
    expect(postRes.status).toBe(200);
    const id = postRes.body.id;
    const getRes = await request(app).get(`/comments/${id}`);
    expect(getRes.status).toBe(200);
    const stored = getRes.body.content;
    // stored content should not contain script tags
    expect(stored).not.toMatch(/<script/i);
    // but should contain allowed <b> tag
    expect(stored).toMatch(/<b>ok<\/b>/i);
  });
});

--- End Integration test example ---

Notes:
- The tests above assume sanitize-html is configured with the same options as the middleware; if you change SANITIZE_OPTIONS, update tests accordingly.
- If you use a separate test DB or persistence layer, adapt the integration test to your setup.
- Consider adding client-side tests to ensure CommentHtml renders sanitized content without creating script elements. For example, use React Testing Library to render and assert no script elements exist in the DOM.

